# MONITORING-LOGS-WATCH-ROOT-CAUSE-1 — logs status truthfulness

Extends [112-monitoring-baseline-zero-known-failures](112-monitoring-baseline-zero-known-failures.mdc).
Rule 112 R4 pinned the fail-closed property for the **disk** half of the snapshot
aggregate. This rule pins the same property for the **logs** half, which is the only
section that answers "is the application healthy" from evidence it has to go and read.

## R1 — A WATCH caused by a real log event is CORRECT, and is never suppressed

`logs = WATCH` means the analyzer counted between 1 and 20 error-like events with a
parsed timestamp inside the lookback window (default `--since=24h`). That is the
monitor working, not the monitor malfunctioning.

The following are forbidden as a means of reaching green, in every combination:

- truncating, deleting, rotating or editing `storage/logs/laravel.log`
- deleting individual log lines, or rewriting an `ERROR` to a lower level
- adding a message-substring allowlist, an exception-class ignore, or any rule keyed
  on the text of a known event
- shortening the lookback window, or raising the `fresh → WATCH` threshold
- disabling the logs section, or dropping it from the `worst()` aggregate

Recovery is by **ageing** — the event leaves the window — or by **fixing the runtime
that emits the error**. There is no third mechanism, and `--since` is a diagnostic
lens, not a remediation.

Evidenced on production during this sprint: the fresh count fell from 5 to 4 between
`2026-08-22T06:07Z` and `2026-08-22T06:18Z` with no intervention, because the oldest
event crossed its 24h boundary at `06:16:25Z`.

## R2 — The logs section never reports OK without evidence

> The logs section may report OK only when it actually read the log and actually
> determined that no error event falls inside the lookback window.
> **Absence of evidence is never evidence of absence.**

Three fail-open paths violated this and are now closed. Each had the same shape: an
unknown was rendered as a clean bill of health.

| Input | Was | Is |
|---|---|---|
| Error event whose header timestamp fails `Carbon::parse` | counted in **no** bucket, `timestamp_parse_status` still `ok` → **OK** | `undated_error_like_count`, parse status `partial`/`failed` → **WATCH** |
| Log file present but unopenable | `readTail` returned `''`, indistinguishable from empty → **OK**, plus a fabricated non-zero `tail_bytes_scanned` | `readTail` returns `null` → **WATCH** + warning, `tail_bytes_scanned = 0` |
| Log file absent | **OK**, silently | **OK**, but always accompanied by a warning and a "not verified" reason |

The absent-file case deliberately keeps status OK. Laravel creates the file on first
write, so absence normally means nothing was logged, and escalating would fire on
every fresh checkout — a permanent false WATCH, which R3 forbids just as firmly. What
may never happen is reporting it *silently*.

`filesize()` and `fopen()` in this path are diagnostics-suppressed (`@`) because their
failure is handled explicitly. Without that the promoted warning aborts the whole
snapshot: a monitor that dies on an unreadable log is strictly worse than one that
reports WATCH and says why.

## R3 — A false WATCH is a defect too

Fail-closed does not license alarm-by-default. A status that can never recover is as
untruthful as one that can never fire. Any new escalation must be shown not to fire on
a healthy, well-formed log, and `PilotPerformanceSnapshotLogSourceTest` carries that
negative control alongside every fail-closed assertion.

The production negative control for this sprint: all 118 timestamped headers in the
live log parse cleanly under a single uniform format, the file is present and
readable, and 775 KB sits far inside the 2 MiB tail budget — so none of the new
escalations can fire there, and production's verdict was unchanged by this sprint.

## R4 — Unparseable noise escalates, it never masks

Severity is driven by the **parsed fresh count**. An undetermined-freshness signal may
only raise the status via `worst()`; it may never short-circuit and discard a count
that was already correct.

The closed defect: 150 fresh events (contractually FIX) alongside 21 orphan lines
returned a flat WATCH, understating a FIX-level burst precisely when the log was
noisiest. Fixed by moving the guard inside the `freshCount === 0` branch.

## R5 — A verdict never claims more window than the scan actually covered

The tail scan is capped at 2 MiB, which is a **byte** budget, not the requested
**time** window. Truncation on its own is not the defect; answering a full-window
question from a partial scan is.

This was a **confirmed false green**, reproduced by execution on the real service with
filler volume as the only variable — the same in-window `SQLSTATE[08006]` error:

```
  13,096 B  (under the cap)  → logs=WATCH   correct
2,600,096 B (over the cap)   → logs=OK      "No fresh error events within lookback window."
                                            fresh=0 orphan=0 parse=ok warnings=(none)
```

The orphan guard cannot rescue it: with `freshCount = 0`, orphan counts of 0/1/5/20 all
return OK, and only ≥21 flips — under an unrelated reason that never mentions coverage.

The window is **covered** when the whole file was read, or when the scanned tail still
reaches back past the cutoff. `oldest_scanned_event_at` (the oldest event of **any**
level the scan reached) is compared against the cutoff to decide
`window_fully_covered`; an uncovered window with nothing countable is **WATCH**, with a
reason that says so, plus a warning naming the remedy (raise the budget, or rotate).

Escalating on truncation *alone* would be wrong — it would fire permanently on any host
writing more than the budget per day, which is R3's false WATCH. That negative control
is pinned: a truncated scan whose tail still starts before the cutoff stays **OK**.

Production writes ~775 KB in total, so the scan is not truncated there and this rule
changes nothing about its current verdict.

## R6 — Operator tooling shares the application log, and monitoring counts it

`php artisan tinker` writes real `ERROR` records into `storage/logs/laravel.log`
(psysh config-directory failures, parse errors from a mistyped `--execute`). Those are
genuine ERROR entries and the monitor is right to count them. Three of the five events
behind this sprint's WATCH were exactly this, one of them created by the investigation
itself, which pushed natural recovery back by roughly 13 hours.

The remedy is operational, never a filter:

- do not run `tinker` against production; prefer a purpose-built read-only command
- the monitor's canonical read is `php artisan pilot:performance-snapshot --json`,
  which is side-effect free
- never add a psysh/tooling suppression rule — that would blind the monitor to the
  next real error that happens to share a phrase

## R7 — The monitoring clock is UTC, not the clinical clock

`PilotPerformanceSnapshotService` passes `now()`, and Monolog writes the header in the
same application timezone, so both sides of the freshness comparison agree. Monitoring
must **not** be moved onto `ClinicalClock`/WITA: that clock exists for clinical "today"
semantics, while a technical instant stays UTC. Report both to humans; compare in one.

Graphify surfaces `ClinicalClock::now()` as a graph neighbour of the analyzer because
both declare `.now()`. That is a naming adjacency, not a dependency — Monitoring
contains zero `ClinicalClock` references.

## R8 — The snapshot is a real production job

`daengtisiams-pilot-snapshot.timer` runs `pilot:performance-snapshot` weekly on
production (`OnCalendar=Sat *-*-* 19:30:00` UTC, `Persistent=true`) as the dedicated
`daengtisiams` runtime identity. A fail-open in this chain is therefore not a
theoretical blemish — it silently produces a green weekly evidence record.

Do not conclude from "no repo script invokes it" that the command is unscheduled; the
timer lives on the host, not in the checkout.
